.TH FUZZYGENE 1 2000-1月 "fuzzygene_trace_perturbation" "fuzzygene_trace_perturbation"
.SH NAME
fuzzygene_trace_perturbation \- Record the whole-genome steady-state trajectory of one virtual perturbation.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fIfuzzygene_trace_perturbation(\fBfuzzygene\fR as <generic> FuzzyGeneModel, 
\fBgene\fR as string, 
\fBmode\fR as string = "knockout", 
\fBsteps\fR as integer = 0, 
[\fB<Environment>\fR]);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Record the whole-genome steady-state trajectory of one virtual perturbation.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBfuzzygene\fB \fR\- a trained (or restored) model. 
.PP
.PP
\fBgene\fB \fR\- the target gene of the virtual perturbation. 
.PP
.PP
\fBmode\fB \fR\- \fBknockout\fR (default), \fBknockdown\fR or \fBoverexpression\fR. 
.PP
.PP
\fBsteps\fB \fR\- maximum iteration steps; 0 uses the model configuration. 
.PP
.PP
\fBenv\fB \fR\- the R# runtime environment used to report errors. 
.PP
.SH VALUE
.PP
a named list of matrices, data.frames and the run summary
.PP
.SH DETAILS
.PP
Returns a named list:
 


 
- \fBmutant\fR: a Matrix (rows = all genes, columns = iteration frames \fBmut_t000\fR...);
- \fBwildtype\fR: the same shape for the unperturbed baseline;
- \fBconvergence\fR: a data.frame \fBiteration\fR / \fBmax_delta\fR of the mutant run;
- \fBcontrol_convergence\fR: the same for the wildtype run;
- \fBchanges\fR: a data.frame \fBgene\fR / \fBfold_change\fR / \fBpercent_change\fR / \fBz_score\fR /
 \fBsignificant\fR of the final state;
- \fBsummary\fR: a one row data.frame with the perturbation definition and the solver diagnostics.


 


 \fBmutant\fR and \fBwildtype\fR are .NET `HTS.DataFrame.Matrix` objects (rows = genes,
 columns = frames), not R matrices, so \fBdim()\fR returns 1 on them. Pass them straight to a
 downstream consumer instead:
 
\fB
 let trace = FuzzyGene::fuzzygene_trace_perturbation(model, "TPPP3");
 geneExpression::write.expr_matrix(trace$mutant, file = "/path/mutant.csv", id = "geneID");
 
\fR`

 


 


 The column index is a solver iteration, not biological time. The default solver
 finds the constrained fixed point of \fBF(z) - z = 0\fR (Levenberg-Marquardt, see
 \fBfuzzygene_hybrid_config(solver = ...)\fR), so the frames are Newton steps and the number of
 frames is small (single digit for a well posed network). Only when the solver falls back to
 the damped iteration does \fBomega\fR shape the curve (\fBfuzzygene_relaxation\fR), and only a
 lagged (time series) model in \fBadvance\fR mode makes one frame equal to one sampling interval.
 


 


 Read \fBconvergence\fR / \fBsummary$converged\fR before interpreting the curve: a flat tail
 produced by running out of \fBsteps\fR is a truncated plateau, not a steady state.
 


 


 \fBwildtype\fR is the control run: it starts from the same baseline and is iterated to its own
 fixed point, so \fBfoldchange\fR is now "mutant fixed point minus wildtype fixed point" instead
 of "mutant minus the training mean". The training mean is generally not a fixed point
 of a nonlinear fuzzy map, so the previous definition mixed the perturbation effect with the
 model's own drift; the wildtype trajectory still exposes that drift, which is worth checking
 (a large drift means the fit is inconsistent with its own steady state).
.PP
